<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Lemko-Rusyn Republic</h1><p class="st-meta">1918 CE – 1920 CE · capital Florynka</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A small Rusyn republic in the Carpathian foothills, proclaimed by the Lemkos in 1918 and suppressed by Poland in 1920.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the collapse of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lemkos">Lemkos</a>, an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-slavs">East Slavic</a> highland people of western <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-galicia-and-lodomeria">Galicia</a>, held a council at Florynka in December 1918. Led by pro-Russian leaders, they refused to join either <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> or the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/west-ukrainian-peoples-republic">West Ukrainian People's Republic</a> and proclaimed their own republic, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaroslav-kachmarchyk">Yaroslav Kachmarchyk</a> as president.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The republic had a small council and local administration among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lemkos">Lemko</a> villages but no real army or economy of its own. Its leaders hoped for union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> or with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rusyns">Rusyns</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> and sent delegates to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris-peace-conference-1919-1920">Paris Peace Conference</a>, without success.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Polish authorities tolerated the republic for a time but ended it in March 1920, arresting its leaders. The region became part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/second-polish-republic">Second Polish Republic</a>, and most <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lemkos">Lemkos</a> were later deported during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> and after it.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/lemko-rusyn-republic.jpg" alt="Lemko-Rusyn Republic" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Losyanyn · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%9F%D1%80%D0%B0%D0%BF%D0%BE%D1%80_%D0%9B%D0%B5%D0%BC%D0%BA%D0%BE-%D0%A0%D1%83%D1%81%D0%B8%D0%BD%D1%81%D1%8C%D0%BA%D0%BE%D1%97_%D0%A0%D0%B5%D1%81%D0%BF%D1%83%D0%B1%D0%BB%D1%96%D0%BA%D0%B8.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1918 CE – 1920 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Florynka</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Yaroslav Kachmarchyk</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek Catholic and Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Rusyn</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ruska Lemkivska Republic, Republic of Florynka</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1919&amp;focus=lemko-rusyn-republic" aria-label="Lemko-Rusyn Republic on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5162 451 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Lemko-Rusyn Republic lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5482 707L5473 630"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5473 630h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5482 707h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5482 707h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Florynka. <a href="/atlas?year=1919&amp;focus=lemko-rusyn-republic">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

